    "https://explore.msujobs.msstate.edu/jobs/research-associate-i-ii-iii-or-senior-main-campus-starkville-ms-mississippi-united-states": {
      "title": "Research Associate I, II, III, or Senior - Main Campus",
      "description": "Research Associate I, II, III, or Senior - Main Campus - Starkville, MS, Mississippi, United States Research Associate I, II, III, or Senior - Main Campus - Starkville, MS, Mississippi, United States Kiosk mode We're Hiring! Research Associate I, II, III, or Senior 511244 Main Campus - Starkville, MS Mississippi - Main Campus 193604 - Athlete Engineering Institute Professional Regular Full-Time 12-month Opening on: Sep 16 2026 Add to favorites View favorites Position Function: The Research Associate serves as the day-to-day technical and operational lead for the Athlete Engineering Institute's human performance laboratory. This position plans and executes biomechanical and physiological data collection across sport, tactical, occupational, and industrial populations; maintains readiness of motion capture, force plate, and wearable sensing systems; and mentors a cross-disciplinary team of student researchers spanning engineering, kinesiology, and other related disciplines. Working under the direction of the Institute Director and alongside faculty, the incumbent translates laboratory capability into sponsored-project deliverables, which can include protocols, processed datasets, prototypes, and reporting. Salary Grade : 13 Please see Staff Compensation Structure or Skilled Crafts and Service Maintenance Compensation Schedule for salary ranges. For salary grade UC, these positions are \"Unclassified\" and salary ranges are determined by the hiring department. Department Profile: The mission of athlete engineering is to align researchers from all engineering disciplines, as well as kinesiology, sociology, and psychology researchers, with athletic practitioners such as strength and conditioning coaches, athletic trainers, and nutritionists, to enhance student-athlete safety and link human factors to performance technology. Essential Duties and Responsibilities: • Lead lab operations: organization, equipment inventory and maintenance, and readiness across active projects. • Plan and execute data collection sessions; train personnel and student workers on lab equipment and protocols. • Mentor and coordinate students from broad disciplinary backgrounds (engineering, kinesiology, fashion/textile design) in research and prototype work. • Facilitate prototype design and iteration in collaboration with student workers and faculty. • Coordinate with IT/HPC on data pipelines, storage, and infrastructure. • Contribute to workforce-preservation activities (human factors, physiology, hydration/heat-illness, occupational health) supporting funded program objectives. • Support reporting, data aggregation, and metrics/dashboarding for sponsored projects. Minimum Qualifications: Grade and salary will be commensurate with individual’s background, education and experience. Beginning with a bachelor’s degree: A professional employee with a bachelor’s degree in engineering, computer science, kinesiology/exercise science, biomedical, or related field and demonstrated competence can begin work at the Research/Extension Associate I level. To begin at the Research/Extension Associate II level, one must have a bachelor’s degree in the required discipline and a minimum of three years relevant experience and the equivalent of a master’s degree. To begin at the Research/Extension Associate III level, one must have a bachelor’s degree in the required discipline and a minimum of six years relevant experience and the equivalent of the master’s degree. Beginning with a master’s degree: A professional employee with a master’s degree in engineering, computer science, kinesiology/exercise science, biomedical, or related field, one year of relevant experience and demonstrated competence can begin work at the Research/Extension Associate II level. To begin at the Research/Extension Associate III level, one must have a master’s degree in the required discipline and a minimum of three years relevant experience and the equivalent of a master’s degree. To begin at the Senior Research/Extension Associate level, one must have a master’s degree in the required discipline and a minimum of six years relevant experience. Beginning with a doctoral degree: A professional employee with a doctoral degree in engineering, computer science, kinesiology/exercise science, biomedical, or related field with two years of relevant experience and demonstrated competence can begin work at the Research/Extension Associate III level. To begin at the Senior Research/Extension Associate level, one must have a doctoral degree in the required discipline and a minimum of three years relevant experience. Preferred Qualifications: • Demonstrated ability to work across engineering and kinesiology-based disciplines: e.g., applying signal processing or sensor integration to physiological/biomechanical questions, or vice versa. • Evidence of self-directed cross-disciplinary learning — a project, publication, or role that bridged two fields outside the candidate's primary training. • Experience mentoring or supervising students from varied backgrounds (engineering, kinesiology, design). Knowledge, Skills, and Abilities: 1. Has established scientific expertise; knows and effectively uses the fundamental concepts, practices, and procedures in human performance and human factors. 2. Ability to organize, interpret data, and derive study conclusions. 3. Ability to advance state-of-the-art scientific research in fields of human performance and human factors. 4. Ability to apply extensive and advanced principles, theories, and concepts. 5. Ability to contribute to the development of solutions to complex problems that require innovation and ingenuity. 6. Project management skills; excellent communication and presentation skills. Working Conditions and Physical Effort Occasional and/or light lifting required up to 10 pounds. Limited exposure to elements such as heat, cold, noise, dust, dirt, etc.; none to the degree of being disagreeable. Frequent standing, walking, climbing or balancing required. Routine deadlines, variance in work volume predictable; priorities can be anticipated; some interruptions are present; involves occasional exposure to demands and pressure from persons other than immediate supervisor. Vision requirements: Ability to see information in print and/or electronically, ability to distinguish colors. Instructions for Applying: Link to apply: http://explore.msujobs.msstate.edu/ Apply online by submitting a cover letter, resume, and a copy of your transcript(s). Any social security numbers included on requested transcripts should be redacted prior to submitting online. Restricted Clause: Position is contingent upon continued availability of funding. Equal Employment Opportunity Statement: Mississippi State University is an equal opportunity institution. Discrimination is prohibited in university employment, programs or activities based on race, color, ethnicity, sex, pregnancy, religion, national origin, disability, age, sexual orientation, genetic information, status as a U.S. veteran, or any other status to the extent protected by applicable law. Questions about equal opportunity programs or compliance should be directed to the Office of Civil Rights Compliance, 231 Famous Maroon Band Street, P.O. 6044, Mississippi State, MS 39762, (662) 325-5839. What do I do if I need an accommodation? In compliance with the ADA Amendments Act (ADA), if you have a disability and would like to request an accommodation in order to apply for a position with Mississippi State University, please contact the Department of Human Resources Management at tel: (662) 325-3713 or ada@hrm.msstate.edu . If you have any questions regarding this policy, contact the Department of Human Resources Management at (662) 325-3713 or ada@hrm.msstate.edu . Upon request, sections of this job listing are available in large print, and readers are available to assist the visually impaired. Back to job listing Apply Now First Name (required) 5d30c820 Last Name (required) 8399c5cd Email (required) ee3185c9 Refer someone to this job Apply Please wait... Done Not You? Thank you Recommended Jobs Extension Agent Walthal County, Mississippi Responsible for providing leadership in the development, implementation, and evaluation of a comprehensive non-formal, experiential educational program in the area of positive youth development (4-H). The Extension Agent serves as a member of the county team to determine effective programming priorities and delivery methods for the community. Research Technician Main Campus - Starkville, MS, Mississippi The research technician will work directly with team leads, undergraduate students and/or recent graduates, and federal and state conservation partners in the southeastern region to review state and regional conservation planning documents and populate and deploy advanced data applications focused on prioritized species conservation and habitat recovery efforts. Clinical Laboratory Manager ***Internal Posting*** Main Campus - Starkville, MS, Mississippi Directs and coordinates activities of employees engaged in performing laboratory tests to obtain data for use in diagnosis and treatment of diseases. Also performs duties of Medical Technologist. Sign Up for Job Alerts Job Alert 6902b1ff Finalize your job alert by selecting criteria from the dropdowns below. You can select multiple options from each dropdown by returning to the combobox and re-entering the list of options. Submit at the end to create your job alert. Categories Professional Professional Employment Type Regular Full-Time 12-month Regular Full-Time 12-month Locations Main Campus - Starkville, MS, Mississippi, United States Main Campus - Starkville, MS, Mississippi, United States First Name (required) 46fc769c Last Name (required) 58613138 Email (required) 77318b7e Send Please wait... Done Not You? Thank you Candidate Details We use cookies Some are necessary to operate the website and its functions. Others 